From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 19:51:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(server): store chat link rules as a validated shared
 preference

Chat messages should turn short references such as BST-2135 or PR #123 into
links, using rules each user sets. The rules run in every viewer's browser on
every message, and agents will be able to write them, so the server checks a
rule before it is stored.

- omnigent/link_rules.py holds the rule shape ({name, kind, pattern, url,
  enabled}, plus provider and host for a git rule), the defaults (GitHub,
  GitLab, Azure DevOps and session references on, two example url rules off)
  and the validator. A url rule's pattern is a short form such as BST-{num}
  or a raw /regex/ limited to a subset Python and every supported browser
  parse alike: no backreferences, lookaround, named groups or inline flags
  (older Safari can't parse named groups or lookbehind), no repeated group
  holding a repeat or |, at most three repeats capped at 64 characters, 200
  characters in all. Its URL template must be http(s) with a fixed scheme and
  host before any placeholder, so a match can't choose where a link goes.
- A git rule stores one git host and its provider (github, gitlab or
  azure_devops), so users can add GitHub Enterprise or a self-managed GitLab.
  The host must be a bare host name with an optional port, with no scheme,
  path or credentials, and a list holds one rule per host. The module also
  picks the rule a session's repository uses (its host, else the provider the
  server resolved), shared with the agent instructions.
- A rule of the earlier github kind reads as the GitHub git rule and is
  stored in the new form on the next write; the validator accepts it on input
  until 0.22.0.
- The rules are the {"kind": "link_rules", "name": "default"} shared
  preference, a JSON array in a string that the preference layer validates
  and stores in a canonical form. null restores the defaults.
- GET /v1/link-rules returns the rules in effect, saved or default, and PUT
  replaces the list with a version check, refusing a stale edit instead of
  storing a conflict. Without shared preferences the defaults are read-only.

Not upstream yet: proposed from feat/chat-link-rules, the git-reference part from
feat/git-ref-notation; drop this copy once they merge.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
